    "data": {
      "text": "===================================================================\nReport ID.....................report02_NOTE\nPatient ID....................PT005678\nPatient Name..................Sample Patient B\nPrincipal Date................20240308 1445\nRecord Type...................MEDICAL ONCOLOGY CONSULTATION\n===================================================================\n[Report de-identified (Limited dataset compliant) by De-ID v.6.24.5.1]\n\nREASON FOR CONSULTATION::\nManagement of metastatic melanoma.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS::\nThis is a 68-year-old male with history of stage IV melanoma originally \ndiagnosed in May 2022. Primary site was right upper back with subsequent \nmetastases to regional lymph nodes and lungs. BRAF V600E mutation positive.\n\nPRIOR TREATMENT HISTORY::\nThe patient underwent wide local excision and sentinel lymph node biopsy \non June 3, 2022. Pathology revealed 2.8 mm Breslow thickness with \nulceration and 2 of 3 sentinel nodes positive.\n\nHe was initially treated with combination immunotherapy consisting of \nNivolumab 1 mg/kg and Ipilimumab 3 mg/kg every 3 weeks. First infusion \nwas on July 20, 2022. He received 4 doses with the last dose on \nOctober 5, 2022. Treatment was complicated by grade 2 colitis requiring \nsteroid therapy, and immunotherapy was discontinued.\n\nDue to progression on imaging 6 months ago (September 2023), he was \nswitched to BRAF/MEK inhibitor combination therapy. He started Dabrafenib \n150 mg PO BID and Trametinib 2 mg PO daily on September 15, 2023. He has \nbeen on this regimen for approximately 6 months with good tolerance.\n\nCURRENT STATUS::\nMost recent CT scan from 2 weeks ago (February 23, 2024) shows partial \nresponse with 40% reduction in target lesions. Patient reports improved \nenergy and resolution of dyspnea. He has experienced mild pyrexia and \noccasional chills, managed with acetaminophen.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION::\nVitals: BP 128/78, HR 72, Temp 98.8\\u00b0F, RR 16\nGeneral: Alert, well-appearing male in no acute distress\nSkin: Well-healed surgical scar on right upper back, no new suspicious lesions\nLymph nodes: No palpable cervical, supraclavicular, or axillary adenopathy\nLungs: Clear to auscultation bilaterally\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN::\n68-year-old male with stage IV BRAF-mutant melanoma demonstrating excellent \nresponse to targeted therapy with Dabrafenib/Trametinib combination.\n\nPlan:\n1. Continue current regimen of Dabrafenib and Trametinib\n2. Repeat CT chest/abdomen/pelvis in 3 months (June 2024)\n3. Monitor for skin toxicity and other adverse effects\n4. Patient counseled on sun protection and self-skin examination\n5. Consider switching back to immunotherapy if disease progression occurs\n\nElectronically signed by: Dr. Jane Smith, MD,\nDate: March 8, 2024 at 3:20 PM\n"
    },
